{"type":"output_text","text":{"value":"Certainly! If you're using the [`webdav`](https://github.com/perry-mitchell/webdav-client) JavaScript/TypeScript client library and want to provide a custom `fetch` implementation (for example, to use a custom agent, add headers, proxy, or some advanced functionality), you can do this via the `createClient` options.

**Here’s a simple example:**  
Suppose you want to use a custom `fetch` function that always logs outgoing requests:

```javascript
import { createClient } from "webdav";

// Custom fetch implementation
const myFetch = async (url, options) => {
    console.log(`[WebDAV] ${options.method || 'GET'} ${url}`);
    // Optionally, modify options here

    // Use the default browser fetch
    return fetch(url, options);
};

// Create client with custom fetch
const client = createClient("https://webdav-server.example.com", {
    username: "your-username",
    password: "your-password",
    fetch: myFetch
});

// Use the client as normal!
async function listRoot() {
    const contents = await client.getDirectoryContents("/");
    console.log(contents);
}

listRoot();
```

**Key points:**
- Your custom fetch must match the [Fetch API signature](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Fetch_API).
- You can use it to set custom headers, cookies, proxies, etc.

**Custom agent (Node.js with `node-fetch`):**

```javascript
import { createClient } from "webdav";
import fetch from "node-fetch";
import HttpsProxyAgent from "https-proxy-agent";

// Create custom fetch using a proxy agent
const agent = new HttpsProxyAgent("http://my-proxy-server:8080");
const myFetch = (url, options = {}) => fetch(url, { ...options, agent });

const client = createClient("https://webdav-server.example.com", {
    username: "user",
    password: "pass",
    fetch: myFetch
});
```

**Reference:**  
